Derive potential differences from work on a fixed probe (@physics-potential-work: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../electromagnetism/electrostatics/Work-potential-difference-and-reference-choice.lingua#r_0KDY19PKDZXWKFN38GPNQMKR3N") { r_0065324DD3782FCD25989AB35C
For finite static point sources in vacuum, define V(r)=kₑΣqᵢ/|r−aᵢ|+V₀ away from the sources. V₀ is an arbitrary common reference. V has units joules per coulomb, called volts. The field is a vector; the potential is a scalar. A zero value of V can be changed by a reference choice and does not imply a zero field. A potential difference between specified points is reference independent.

The work connection can be proved using only derivatives of a parametrized path. Let r(s) be a continuously differentiable path that does not hit a source; piecewise smooth paths are treated segment by segment. For a fixed a, set d(s)=r(s)−a and R(s)=√(d·d)>0. Differentiating the finite sum of coordinate squares gives R′=d·r′/R and (1/R)′=−d·r′/R³. Therefore dV(r(s))/ds=−E(r(s))·r′(s). Define the work integral on fixed probe qₜ by ∫qₜE(r(s))·r′(s) ds. The fundamental theorem yields W_field=qₜ[V(start)−V(end)]. This proof uses the explicit finite-source potential; it does not assume a general vector-calculus theorem.

The probe's potential energy change is ΔU=qₜ[V(end)−V(start)], so W_field=−ΔU. If only this electric force does work on a free probe, ΔK=W_field. If an external agent moves it with negligible change of kinetic energy, that agent does work ΔU instead. State which system and agent the energy belongs to. The fixed-source construction excludes source motion and induced changes; an interacting many-body energy account must include them consistently.

For the axial sources from [Build a finite electric field from signed vector contributions](Build-a-finite-electric-field-from-signed-vector-contributions.lingua#r_0024617F87039DB1C727C66540), the scaled potential on 0<ξ<3 is φ(ξ)=2/ξ−1/(3−ξ), with the reference at infinity set to zero. At ξ=1, φ=3/2; at ξ=2, φ=0, even though the field there is 3/2 in field units. A probe qₜ=Q*/100 moved from L to 2L receives field work (3/200)kₑQ*²/L. Reversing the probe sign or reversing the path reverses that work. The agent maintaining a quasistatic move has the opposite work, under the same stipulated source conditions.

A real voltage comparison needs a stated second point. At ξ=4, φ=−1/2. The dimensionless differences φ(1)−φ(4)=2 and φ(2)−φ(4)=1/2 survive any added V₀. For two unknown scaled charges α at 0 and β at 3, these differences obey d₁=3α/4−β/2 and d₂=α/4. They determine α=4d₂ and β=6d₂−2d₁ within that fixed two-source model. One difference alone leaves a family. Additional unmodeled charges or changed geometry make a different inverse problem.

**Work and reading:** prove the path derivative and reconstruct work along two source-avoiding paths with the same endpoints. Explain why a path through a point singularity is outside the proof. Verify the signed probe energy and the two-source inverse with [Reduce rectangular constraints with reversible row operations](../../mathematics/core/Reduce rectangular constraints with reversible row operations.lingua#r_0040C3CC99E7DD621AA0C570AA). Read [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) sections 7.2–7.3 and the bounded common part of [Work, potential difference and reference choice](../electromagnetism/electrostatics/Work-potential-difference-and-reference-choice.lingua#r_0KDY19PKDZXWKFN38GPNQMKR3N). Time-dependent magnetic induction can produce a nonconservative electric field; the single static potential account does not claim to cover that regime. General gradient, flux and curl theory remains future common preparation.

**Continue:** [Work, potential difference and reference choice](../electromagnetism/electrostatics/Work-potential-difference-and-reference-choice.lingua#r_0KDY19PKDZXWKFN38GPNQMKR3N).

